          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %strong exact sequence
            may be defined in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-abelian+category"} semi-abelian category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+category"} homological category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+diagram"} sequential diagram
            in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            of each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            is equal to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            of the next morphism.
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %h3#definition_in_additive_categories Definition in additive categories
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive category
            (often assumed to be an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo =
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} = R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            some
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
          #ExactSequence.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong exact sequence
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              with vanishing
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
              in each degree:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\forall n \in \mathbb{N} . H_n(C) = 0
                    \\,.
          #ShortExactSequence.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong short exact sequence
              is an exact sequence, def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExactSequence"}
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\cdots \to 0 \to 0 \to A \to B \to C \to 0 \to 0 \to \cdots
                    \\,.
            %p
              One usually writes this just “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to A \to B \to C \to 0
              ” or even just “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B \to C
              ”.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              A general exact sequence is sometimes called a
              = succeed "," do
                %strong long exact sequence
          #CharacterizationByMonoEpi.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p Explicitly, a sequence of morphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    0 \to A \stackrel{i}\to B \stackrel{p}\to C  \to 0
            %p
              is short exact, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ShortExactSequence"}
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  is an
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              %li
                %p
                  and the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  equals the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  (equivalently, the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coimage"} coimage
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  equals the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
                  of
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The third condition is the definition of exactness at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %p
              This is easy to see by looking at elements when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} \simeq R
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              (and the general case can be reduced to this one using one of the
              = succeed "):" do
                %a{:href => "abelian%20category#EmbeddingTheorems"} embedding theorems
            %p The sequence being exact at
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    0 \to A \to B
            %p
              means, since the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to A
              is just the element
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
              consists of just this element. But since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphism"} group homomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            %p Dually, the sequence being exact at
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B \to C \to 0
            %p
              means, since the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to 0
              is all of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to C
              is all of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to C
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+exact+sequence"} split exact sequence
              is a short exact sequence as above in which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+monomorphism"} split monomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+epimorphism"} split epimorphism
          %p
            In this case,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            may be decomposed as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproduct"} biproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \oplus C
            (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            the usual biproduct inclusion and projection); this sense in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is ‘split’ into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the origin of the general terms ‘split (mono/epi)morphism’.
          %h3#definition_in_pointed_sets Definition in pointed sets
          %p
            It is also helpful to consider a similar notion in the case of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+set"} pointed set
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              In the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Set
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_*
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"} pointed sets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo ,
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi C
                            %mo ,
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    (A, a) &amp; \overset{f}{\to} &amp; (B, b) &amp; \overset{g}{\to} &amp; (C, c)
                    }
            %p
              is said to be
              %strong exact
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B, b)
              if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi im
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im f = g^{-1}(c)
            %p
              For
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete
              pointed categories (ie. a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              with a faithful functor
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Set
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: \mathcal{C} \to Set_*
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is exact.
          %p
            In the case of (abelian) categories like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo −
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R-Mod
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequence+of+homotopy+groups"} long exact sequence of homotopy groups
            where the homotopy “groups” for small
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            are just pointed sets without a group structure.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#computing_terms_in_an_exact_sequence Computing terms in an exact sequence
          %p
            A typical use of a long exact sequence, notably of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+long+exact+sequence"} homology long exact sequence
          %p
            The characterization of short exact sequences in prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CharacterizationByMonoEpi"}
            is one example for this: whenever in a long exact sequence one entry vanishes as in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot \to 0 \to C_n \to \cdot
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot \to C_n \to 0 \to \cdots
            %em next
            morphism out of or into the vanishing entry is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
          %p In particular:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p If part of an exact sequence looks like
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\cdots \to 0 \to C_{n+1} \stackrel{\partial_n}{\to} C_n \to 0 \to \cdots
                    \\,,
            %p
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_n
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              and hence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C_{n+1} \simeq C_n
                    \\,.
          %h3#ExactnessAndQuasiIsomorphisms Exactness and quasi-isomorphisms
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              A chain complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet
              is exact (is a long exact sequence), precisely if the unique
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+map"} chain map
              from the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    0 \to C_\bullet
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
          %h3#SESAndQuotients Short exact sequences and quotients
          %p
            The following are some basic lemmas that show how given a long exact sequence one obtains short exact sequences from forming
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotients"} quotients
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernels"} cokernels
          %p
          #TruncatingExactSequences.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p For an exact sequence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A_{-2}
                    \\overset{f_{-2}}{\longrightarrow}
                    A_{-1}
                    \\overset{f_{-1}}{\longrightarrow}
                    A_0
                    \\overset{f_1}{\longrightarrow}
                    A_1
                    \\overset{f_2}{\longrightarrow}
                    A_2
            %p
